vent Cancer Foundation invests in programming and grants to:\nL65: \nL66:   * share information with the public about how some cancers can be prevented through healthy lifestyle choices and medical screenings.\nL67:   * increase cancer screening and vaccination rates.\nL68: ## Achieving Better Health Outcomes with All Populations\nL69: \nL70: 【28†Community Grants Program】 【48†Image: Two women wearing masks stand at a mammogram machine. One of them, a health care provider, helps the other, a patient dressed in a medical gown, get into position for her breast cancer screening.】 【48†Image: Two women wearing masks stand at a mammogram machine. One of them, a health care provider, helps the other, a patient dressed in a medical gown, get into position for her breast cancer screening.】\nL71: #### Community Grants Program\nL72: \nL73: Prevent Cancer Foundation’s Community Grants Program supports local cancer awareness education, screening and vaccination programs across the United States for all populations, especially medically underserved communities.\nL74: 【29†Congressional Families Program】 【49†Image: Logo with Capitol building and horizontal helix. The logo text reads: Congressional Families, Program of the Prevent Cancer Foundation】 【49†Image: Logo with Capitol building and horizontal helix. The logo text reads: Congressional Families, Program of the Prevent Cancer Foundation】\nL75: #### Congressional Families Program\nL76: \nL77: The Congressional Families® Program is a bipartisan, nonpolitical network of members of Congress and their spouses/partners who share an interest in advancing cancer prevention and early detection through outreach and education.\nL78: 【30†Global Impact】 【50†Image: In a clinic in India, an elderly man is seated across from a health care provider and is having his blood pressure read by a woman in a turquoise sari. Behind them is a desk where another provider is seated and speaking to a woman standing. They are reviewing a document together.】 【50†Image: In a clinic in India, an elderly man is seated across from a health care provider and is having his blood pressure read by a woman in a turquoise sari.\nL79: Behind them is a desk where another provider is seated and speaking to a woman standing. They are reviewing a document together.】\nL80: #### Global Impact\nL81: \nL82: The Foundation’s reach stretches beyond the U.S. and into dozens of countries, territories and commonwealths around the globe.\nL83: 【32†Too Young for This Sh*t!】 【51†Image: A man in his 40s wearing dress clothes is facing away from the camera in front of a toilet in a stall, holding a roll of toilet paper in his right hand. There is text painted on the wall tiles next to the toilet that reads, “Too Young for This Sh*t!”】 【51†Image: A man in his 40s wearing dress clothes is facing away from the camera in front of a toilet in a stall, holding a roll of toilet paper in his right hand.\nL84: There is text painted on the wall tiles next to the toilet that reads, “Too Young for This Sh*t!”】\nL85: #### Too Young for This Sh*t!\nL86: \nL87: You may think you’re too young for this sh*t, but colorectal cancer is on the rise in younger adults.\nL88: 【33†Breast Health Education Guide】 【52†Image: Four young women of different ethnicities gather with their arms around each other’s shoulders. They appear to be outdoors with the wind blowing their long hair. All four are laughing.】 【52†Image: Four young women of different ethnicities gather with their arms around each other’s shoulders. They appear to be outdoors with the wind blowing their long hair. All four are laughing.】\nL89: #### Breast Health Education Guide\nL90: \nL91: The Foundation’s Breast Health Education for Young Women Facilitator’s Guide is designed to increase young women’s knowledge about breast health.\nL92: \nL93: 【34†2026 Early Detection Survey Results】 【53†Image: Stethoscope resting on a medical chart】 【53†Image: Stethoscope resting on a medical chart】\nL94: #### 2026 Early Detection Survey Results\nL95: \nL96: The Prevent Cancer Foundation’s annual Early Detection Survey asks U.S. adults about their knowledge and behaviors around routine cancer screening.\nL97: ### Prevention news for you\nL98: \nL99: \"*\" indicates required fields\nL100: \nL101: [Input]\nL102: \nL103: Email*\nL104: \nL105: [Input: Email Address]\nL106: \nL107: [Button: Submit] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input]\nL108: \nL109: This site is protected by reCAPTCHA and the Google 【54†Privacy Policy†policies.google.com】 and 【55†Terms of Service†policies.google.com】 apply.\nL110: \nL111:   * Contact:\nL112:   * pcf@preventcancer.org\nL113:   * (703) 836-4412\nL114: 333 John Carlyle Street, Suite 635\nL115: Alexandria, VA 22314\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCancer Prevention and Early Detection Community Grants (https://preventcancer.org/education-outreach/community-grants/)\nciteturn2view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://preventcancer.org/education-outreach/community-grants/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 261\nL0: 【0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Prevent Cancer Foundation】\nL3: \nL4:   * 【2†Skip to navigation Accesskey \"n\"】\nL5:   * 【3†Skip to content Accesskey \"c\"】\nL6:   * 【4†Skip to footer Accesskey \"f\"】\nL7: \nL8: 【5†Search】\nL9: \nL10: 【6†Menu】\nL11: \nL12: 【7†Donate】\nL13:   * 【8†Prevention & Screening】 [2]\nL14:     * 【9†Ways to Prevent Cancer】\nL15:     * 【10†Cancer Screenings and Prevention】\nL16:     * 【11†Free and Low-Cost Cancer Screenings, Testing and Vaccinations】\nL17:     * 【12†Make Your Screening Plan】\nL18:     * 【13†Know Your Family History】\nL19:     * 【14†Viruses and Cancer】\nL20:     * 【15†Resources and Downloads】\nL21:   * 【16†By Cancer Type】 [10]\nL22: \nL23: We're here to help you navigate cancer prevention and early detection. Select a cancer type to learn more.\nL24:     * 【17†Breast Cancer】\nL25:     * 【18†Cervical Cancer】\nL26:     * 【19†Colorectal Cancer】\nL27:     * 【20†Liver Cancer】\nL28:     * 【21†Lung Cancer】\nL29:     * 【22†Oral Cancer】\nL30:     * 【23†Prostate Cancer】\nL31:     * 【24†Skin Cancer】\nL32:     * 【25†Testicular Cancer】\nL33:   * 【26†Education & Community】 [11]\nL34:     * 【27†Impact Grants】\nL35:     * 【28†Community Grants】\nL36:     * 【29†Congressional Families Program】\nL37:     * 【30†Global Impact】\nL38:     * 【31†Community Exchange】\nL39:     * 【32†Too Young for This Sh*t】\nL40:     * 【33†Breast Health Education Guide】\nL41:     * 【8†Early Detection = Better Outcomes】\nL42:     * 【34†2026 Early Detection Survey】\nL43:     * 【35†Cancer Prevention and Early Detection Month】\nL44:   * 【36†Research】 [22]\nL45:   * 【37†Policy & Advocacy】 [23]\nL46:     * 【38†2025 Future of Cancer Screening】\nL47:     * 【39†Policy and Advocacy Resources】\nL48:     * 【40†Multi-Cancer Early Detection】\nL49:     * 【41†Take Action】\nL50:   * 【42†About Us】\nL51:   * 【43†The Latest】\nL52:   * 【44†Events】\nL53:   * 【45†Ways to Give】\nL54:   * 【46†Newsletter Signup】\nL55:   * English [Select]\nL56: \nL57: 【16†Close】\nL58: \nL59: [Input: Search] Search\nL60: \nL61: 【47†Image: A doctor of Korean descent bows and shakes hands with an elderly man also of Korean descent.】 【47†Image: A doctor of Korean descent bows and shakes hands with an elderly man also of Korean descent.】 Photo courtesy of the Korean Health Education Information and Research Center.\nL62: \nL63: EDUCATION & COMMUNITY\nL64: # Community Grants\nL65: \nL66: Organizations across the country are doing extraordinary work to get medically under-resourced communities the education, screenings and vaccinations they need to prevent cancer or detect it early.\nL67: \nL68: The call for 2027 Impact Grant applications is now closed. Applicants will be notified of funding decisions on December 4, 2026.\nL69: \nL70: * * *\nL71: ## 2026 Patient Navigation Grantees\nL72: \nL73: To advance the Prevent Cancer Foundation’s bold goal to reduce cancer deaths by 40% by 2035, the Foundation is proud to support 13 projects dedicated to increasing cancer prevention and early detection through patient navigation across the U.S. and its territories, ranging from Santa Rosa, California to Guaynabo, Puerto Rico to Washington, D.C.\nL74: Each program will receive $100,000 over two years. Since 2007, the Prevent Cancer Foundation has awarded over 130 grants to outstanding projects in 38 states, Washington D.C., Puerto Rico, and American Samoa and to the Washoe Tribe. The community grants are generously supported by the Awesome Games Done Quick community and by Barbara Allen in honor of her husband, Ray Allen. Learn more about these diverse projects below.\nL75: 【48†Image: A senior man of Asian decent is seated and reviewing paperwork on a clipboard. A woman of Asian decent in her 20s stand next to the man and is pointing to something on the clipboard.】 【48†Image: A senior man of Asian decent is seated and reviewing paperwork on a clipboard. A woman of Asian decent in her 20s stand next to the man and is pointing to something on the clipboard.】 Health Betterment Initiative, Washington, D.C.\nL76: 【49†Image: A information table is set up outdoors with a giant inflatable lung set up around it. A white man is standing behind the table talking to three people who are facing away from the camera.】 【49†Image: A information table is set up outdoors with a giant inflatable lung set up around it. A white man is standing behind the table talking to three people who are facing away from the camera.】 Nevada Cancer Coalition, Reno, Nevada\nL77: 【50†Image: A senior Latina woman is seated in a clinic. She is wearing a mask and is about to get a vaccine. A health care provider is seated next her just out of focus and preparing a bandage.】 【50†Image: A senior Latina woman is seated in a clinic. She is wearing a mask and is about to get a vaccine. A health care provider is seated next her just out of focus and preparing a bandage.】 VOCES Coalición de Inmunización y Promoción de la Salud, Guaynabo, Puerto Rico\nL78: #### Canoncito Band of Navajos Health Center, Inc.\nL79: \nL80: Location: To’hajiilee, New Mexico\nL81: Cancers: Breast, cervical and colorectal\nL82: Canoncito Band of Navajos Health Center aims to increase colorectal, cervical and breast cancer screening rates. Patient navigators will provide culturally tailored education to help patients access screenings, receive follow-up care and navigate the health care system. They hope to reach 500 people for colorectal cancer screening and 250 women for cervical and breast cancer screening.\nL83: The project will integrate traditional Navajo values with modern medical practices to improve and increase community cancer prevention awareness and health outcomes.\nL84: 2026 Prevent Cancer Dialogue Poster:\nL85: 【51†Cancer Prevention Awareness†app.monstercampaigns.com】\nL86: #### Health Betterment Initiative\nL87: \nL88: Location: Washington, D.C.\nL89: Cancer: Liver\nL90: Health Betterment Initiative (HBI) will employ Community Health Workers and Linkage to Care Specialists to provide culturally and linguistically appropriate navigation services, liver health education and hepatitis screenings. HBI aims to engage at least 3,000 people through in-person education and screen at least 1,600 of them.\nL91: By facilitating early detection through education and screening and guiding people through the health care system, the project aims to reduce liver cancer cases and improve health outcomes in underserved populations in the D.C. metro area.\nL92: 2026 Prevent Cancer Dialogue Poster:\nL93: 【52†Advancing Liver Cancer Prevention Through Community Screening and Patient Navigation in Washington DC, Maryland, and Virginia†app.monstercampaigns.com】\nL94: #### Horizons Community Solutions, Inc.\nL95: \nL96: Location: Albany, Georgia\nL97: Cancer: Lung\n"
